  "verdict": "A longer window is not established as better: 240h minus 48h is 0.0070R in training, -0.0126R in validation and 0.0314R sealed, and the sign flips. Two things carry the change anyway. The ladder moves the stop to entry once TP1 is banked, so past that point a longer hold cannot lose and can only convert to TP2 or TP3 — and at 48h, 46.3% of everything cut off had already banked TP1. And engine-sealed-test.mjs, whose result this site publishes as the engine on data it never saw, allows 240 hours, so shipping 48 meant the headline number described a system given five times as long. Tools here are not unanimous — six use 48 and eight use 240 — which is itself the thing to fix. Note the cost model charges spread only: financing on a multi-day hold is not modelled, so the longer windows above are optimistic by an amount not yet measured."
